%[text] %[text:anchor:T_484c] # Supervised Learning - Week 3 / Part 3
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6ff6] ## Simplified Cost Function
%[text] In the previous file we saw the loss function and the cost function for the logistic regression. In this file we will see a simpler way to write the loss and cost functions for a logistic regression task. Therefore, the implementation will be simpler when we need to implement logistic regression using gradient descent (GD).
%[text] As a reminder, the following is the loss function for the logistic regression,
%[text] $L\\left( y^{(i)}, f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) = \n\\left\\{\n    \\begin{array}{lr}\n        -\\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right), & \\text{if } y^{(i)} = 1  \\\\\n        -\\log\\left( 1 - f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right), & \\text{if } y^{(i)} = 0\n    \\end{array}$
%[text] Since $y$ can only take the values 0 or 1 and nothing else, we will be able to come up with a simpler expression. We can write the loss function as follows:
%[text] $L\\left( y^{(i)}, f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) = -(1-y^{(i)}) \\log \\left( 1 - f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) - y^{(i)}\\log \\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right)$
%[text] Using the simplified loss function definition above, we can write a simplified cost function expression as follows:
%[text] $J(\\mathbf{w},b) = \\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} L \\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} (\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}), y^{(i)}  \\right) = -\\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( \\left( 1-y^{(i)} \\right)\\log\\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) + y^{(i)}\\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg)$
%[text] An important question this point is that "Why did we chose this particular loss function, where there are plenty of other alternative loss functions out there?"
%[text] This particular loss function is derived using **maximum likelihood estimation (MLE)**. Also, this cost function has good properties of being convex, which is crucial for the GD implementation.
%[text]  In the upcoing lab, we will see how the logistic regression cost function is implemented. Moreober, we will also see that how two different selection of a parameter leads to two different loss functions.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_05f0] ## Optional Lab: Cost Function for Logistic Regression
%[text] Let's use the data set that we used while working on the decision boundaries
X_train = [0.5, 1.5; 1, 1; 1.5, 0.5; 3, 0.5; 2, 2; 1, 2.5];
y_train = [0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1];
%[text] Let's plot the data set
% Plot data set
figure; %[output:9984c97d]
hBinaryScatter(X_train, y_train, {'o','x'}, {'b','r'}, 8); %[output:9984c97d]
legend('y = 1','','','y = 0','','','Location','northeast'); %[output:9984c97d]
xlabel('x_0'); %[output:9984c97d]
ylabel('x_1'); %[output:9984c97d]
grid on; %[output:9984c97d]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2da3] ### Cost Function
%[text] $J(\\mathbf{w},b) = \\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} L \\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} (\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}), y^{(i)}  \\right) = -\\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( \\left( 1-y^{(i)} \\right)\\log\\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) + y^{(i)}\\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg)$
%[text] where $m$ is the number of training samples. Moreover,
%[text] $ f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) = g(z^{(i)}) = g(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)$, where $g(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b) = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b}}$
function J = computeLogisticRegressionCost(X, y, w, b)
    % Arguments:
    % X (m,n) : Data, m examples with n features
    % y (m,1) : Target values
    % w (n,1) : model weights
    % b (1,1) : model bias
    m = size(X,1);
    fwb = zeros(m,1);
    J_tmp = 0;
    
    for i = 1:m
        z = X(i,:)*w + b;
        fwb(i) = ( 1/(1+exp(-z)) );
        
        J_tmp = J_tmp + ( (1-y(i))*log(1-fwb(i)) + y(i)*log(fwb(i)) );
    end
    J = -(1/m)*J_tmp;
end
%[text] Test the implemented of the cost function, (**Expected output**: 0.3668667864055175)
expected_val = 0.3668667864055175;
w_tmp = [1 1].';
b_tmp = -3;
J = computeLogisticRegressionCost(X_train, y_train, w_tmp, b_tmp);
fprintf('Cost (J): %.10G\n', J); %[output:3db54755]
fprintf('Squared Error (actual vs expected): %.8G\n', (J-expected_val)^2); %[output:8e968c14]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4e59] ### Example
%[text] Now since we tested the logistic regression cost function, we can examine the cost function output for different $\\mathbf{w}$ and $b$.
%[text] Let's plot the decision boundary for $w\_0=1$, $w\_1=1$, $b = -3$ (shown in magenta), $x\_0 + x\_1 - 3 = 0$
%[text]  and $w\_0=1$, $w\_1=1$, $b = -4$ (shown in cyan), $x\_0 + x\_1 - 4 = 0$.
x0 = linspace(0,4);

figure; %[output:1c4e297c]
hBinaryScatter(X_train, y_train, {'o','x'}, {'b','r'}, 8); %[output:1c4e297c]
hold on; %[output:1c4e297c]
plot(x0,3-x0,'c','LineWidth',1.5); %[output:1c4e297c]
plot(x0,4-x0,'m','LineWidth',1.5); %[output:1c4e297c]
legend('y = 0','','','y = 1','','','b = -3','b = -4','Location','northeast'); %[output:1c4e297c]
xlabel('x_0'); %[output:1c4e297c]
ylabel('x_1'); %[output:1c4e297c]
grid on; %[output:1c4e297c]
hold off; %[output:1c4e297c]
ylim([0 4]) %[output:1c4e297c]
%[text] We can see from the above plot that $b=-4$ is a worse model for training data. Let's examine if the loss function reflects this characteristics.
J1 = computeLogisticRegressionCost(X_train, y_train, [1 1].', -3);
fprintf('Cost for b = -3 (J): %.10G\n', J1); %[output:9c002067]
J2 = computeLogisticRegressionCost(X_train, y_train, [1 1].', -4);
fprintf('Cost for b = -4 (J): %.10G\n', J2); %[output:81122e3f]
%[text] We can see from the above results that the cost function behaves as expected, where the cost function for $b = -4$ case is higher than $b = -3$ case.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_295f] ## Gradient Descent Implementation
%[text] We will take a look at how to find a good choice for the parameters $\\mathbf{w}$ and $b$ that minimizes the cost function $J(\\mathbf{w},b)$ using gradient descent (GD). The objective could be summarized as follows:
%[text] - Find the parameters $\\mathbf{w}$, $b$
%[text] - Given new input ($\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}$) that outputs $f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\frac{1}{1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}}$
%[text] - Then, model make a prediction $P(y=1 \~ | \~ \\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\~;\~\\mathbf{w},b)$
%[text] - In the mean time, the algorithm will try to minimizing the cost function ($J(\\mathbf{w},b)$) to increase the prediction quality. \
%[text] $J(\\mathbf{w},b) = \\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} L \\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} (\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}), y^{(i)}  \\right) = -\\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( y^{(i)}\\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) + \\left( 1-y^{(i)} \\right)\\log\\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg)$
%[text] - If we want to minimize the cost function $J(\\mathbf{w},b)$, we will use the usual GD algorithm: \
%[text] repeat {
%[text] $w\_j = w\_j - \\alpha\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial w\_j} \~,$
%[text] $b = b - \\alpha\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial b}$
%[text] }
%[text] where $j \\in \\{1,2,\\cdots,n\\}$ and $n$ represents the number of features. You repetively update the parameters $\\mathbf{w}$ and $b$ based on the derivative of the cost function, $J(\\mathbf{w},b)$, with respect to the parameters. Let's calculate the terms $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial w\_j}$ and $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial b}$ to implement the GD algorithm.
%[text] - Let's start with $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial w\_j}$ \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_37a7] ### Partial derivative of the cost function w.r.t weights
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial w\_j}\n\n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j} \\bigg( -\\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( y^{(i)}\\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) + \\left( 1-y^{(i)} \\right)\\log\\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg) \\bigg) \\\\\n\n= -\\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j} \\left( y^{(i)}\\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\right) + \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j} \\left( \\left( 1-y^{(i)} \\right)\\log\\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\right) \\bigg).$
%[text] - Let's focus on the first term of the sum, \
%[text]  $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j} \\bigg( y^{(i)}\\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg)\n\n= y^{(i)}  \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j} \\bigg( \\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg)\n\n= y^{(i)} \\cdot \\frac{\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)})}{f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)})} \\quad ,$
%[text] where we know that
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j} \\bigg( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\bigg) \n\n= x\_j^{(i)} \\cdot f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big( 1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big)$, where the proof is provided in Appendix 1.
%[text] Hence,
%[text]  $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j} \\bigg( y^{(i)}\\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg)\n\n= y^{(i)} \\bigg( \\frac{\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)})}{f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)})} \\bigg)\n\n=  y^{(i)} \\bigg( \\frac{x\_j^{(i)} \\cdot f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big( 1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big)}{f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)})} \\bigg)\n\n= y^{(i)} \\bigg( x\_j^{(i)} \\cdot \\big( 1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big) \\bigg) \\quad \\\#$
%[text] - Let's focus on the second term of the sum. Based on the product rule and derivative of a $\\log()$ function, \
%[text] $\\frac{d}{dx} \\left( u \\cdot v \\right) = \\frac{du}{dx} \\cdot v + u \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dx}$ and
%[text]  $\\frac{d}{dx}\\log\_{a} f(x) = \\frac{1}{f(x)\\ln(a)} \\cdot \\left( \\frac{d}{dx}f(x) \\right)$
%[text] We can obtain the following,
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j} \\bigg( \\left(1-y^{(i)}\\right) \\log\\left( 1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg) \n\n= 0 \\cdot  \\log\\left( 1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) + \\left(1-y^{(i)}\\right) \\cdot  \\bigg( \\frac{1}{ 1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) } \\cdot \\frac{d}{dw\_j} \\left( 1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg) \\\\\n\n= \\frac{1-y^{(i)}}{1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)})} \\cdot \\bigg( -x\_j^{(i)} \\cdot f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big( 1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big) \\bigg)\n\n=  \\left( y^{(i)}-1 \\right) \\cdot x\_j^{(i)} \\cdot f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)})  \\quad \\\#\n$
%[text] - If we combine the parts 1 and 2, \
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial w\_j} = -\\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( y^{(i)} \\cdot x\_j^{(i)} \\cdot \\big( 1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big) + \n \\left( y^{(i)}-1 \\right) \\cdot x\_j^{(i)} \\cdot f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\bigg) \\\\\n\n= -\\frac{1}{m}\\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( y^{(i)}x\_j^{(i)} - y^{(i)}x\_j^{(i)}f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) + y^{(i)}x\_j^{(i)}f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) - x\_j^{(i)}f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\bigg)$
%[text] Finally,
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial w\_j} =  \\frac{1}{m}\\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( x\_j^{(i)} \\left(f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) -  y^{(i)} \\right) \\bigg) \\quad \\\#$
%[text] In other words,
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial w\_j} =  \\frac{1}{m}\\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( x\_j^{(i)} \\cdot e^{(i)} \\bigg) \~ ,$
%[text] where $e^{(i)}$ represents the error term for the $i^\\text{th}$ sample, $e^{(i)} = f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) -  y^{(i)}$.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0ac8] ### Partial derivative of the cost function w.r.t bias
%[text] Now, let's also look at the term $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial b}$
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial b}\n\n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\bigg( -\\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( y^{(i)}\\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) + \\left( 1-y^{(i)} \\right)\\log\\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg) \\bigg) \\\\\n\n= -\\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\bigg( y^{(i)}\\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg) +\n\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\bigg(  \\left( 1-y^{(i)} \\right)\\log\\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg) \\bigg)$
%[text] - Let's focus on the first term, \
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\bigg( y^{(i)}\\log(f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} )) \\bigg)\n\n= 0 \\cdot \\log(f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} )) + y^{(i)} \\cdot \\frac{\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} ) \\right)}{f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} )} \\\\\n\n= \\frac{y^{(i)}}{f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right)} \\cdot \\bigg(  f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\cdot \\big( 1 - f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big) \\bigg)\n\n= y^{(i)} \\cdot \\big( 1 - f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big)$
%[text] - Now, we can focus on the second term, \
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\bigg(  \\left( 1-y^{(i)} \\right)\\log\\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg) \\bigg) \n\n= 0 \\cdot \\log\\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) + (1-y^{(i)}) \\cdot \\bigg( \\frac{1}{1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) } \\cdot \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg) \\\\\n\n= \\frac{\\left(  1 - y^{(i)} \\right)}{\\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right)} \\cdot \\bigg(  -f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\cdot \\big( 1 - f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big) \\bigg) \n\n= \\left( y^{(i)} - 1 \\right) \\cdot f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)})$
%[text] - If we combine both parts, \
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial b}\n\n= -\\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\bigg( y^{(i)}\\log\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg) +\n\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\bigg(  \\left( 1-y^{(i)} \\right)\\log\\left(1-f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg) \\bigg) \\\\\n\n= -\\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( y^{(i)} \\cdot \\big( 1 - f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big) + \\left( y^{(i)} - 1 \\right) \\cdot f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\bigg)$
%[text] Finally,
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial b}\n\n=  \\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) - y^{(i)} \\bigg) \\quad \\\#$
%[text] In other words,
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial b}\n\n=  \\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} e^{(i)} \~,$ 
%[text] where $e^{(i)}$ represents the error term for the $i^\\text{th}$ sample, $e^{(i)} = f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) -  y^{(i)}$.
%[text] **Please note that similar to the linear regression task, the way to update the parameters has to be simultaneous in order not to affect the gradients.** 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6760] - Simultaneous update means that we would calculate the right hand side of each expression, $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w}, b)}{\\partial w\_j} $ and $\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w}, b)}{\\partial b} $ first.
%[text] - And then simultaneously calculate and overwrite, $w\_j = w\_j - \\alpha\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial w\_j}$ and $b = b - \\alpha\\frac{\\partial J(\\mathbf{w},b)}{\\partial b}$. \
%[text] Consequently the gradient descent (GD) for logistic regression task becomes,
%[text] - Randomly initialize the parameters: $\\mathbf{w}$, and $b$.
%[text] - Repeat the following expression until the condition is satisfied; (i) Minimum error has been reached, or (ii) maximum number of iterations has been reached. \
%[text] $w\_j = w\_j - \\alpha\\frac{1}{m}\\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( \\left(f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) -  y^{(i)} \\right) \\cdot x\_j^{(i)}  \\bigg) \\quad (1)$
%[text] $b = b - \\alpha \\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) - y^{(i)} \\bigg) \\quad (2)$
%[text] **Now, one interesting thing to note here is that the GD algorithm for the linear regression task was also the same.**
%[text] $w\_j \n\n= w\_j - \\alpha\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}J(\\mathbf{w},b)\n\n=  w\_j - \\alpha \\left( \\frac{1}{m}\\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) - y^{(i)} \\right) \\cdot\nx\_j^{(i)} \\right)  \n\n\\quad (3)$
%[text] $b \n\n= b - \\alpha\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b}J(\\mathbf{w},b)\n\n= b - \\alpha \\left( \\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) - y^{(i)}  \\right) \\right)\n\n\\quad (4)$ 
%[text] for $i \\in \\{ 1,2,\\cdots,m\\}$ and $j \\in \\{ 1,2,\\cdots, n\\}$. 
%[text] 
%[text] Obviously, the definition of the function $f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right)$ is different for the linear and logistic regression tasks:
%[text] - **Linear regression:** $f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) = \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\mathbf{w} +b$
%[text] - **Logistic regression:** $f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) = \\frac{1}{1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}}$ \
%[text] Although the general GD algorithm is the same in the high level, the output function in each task is different from each other.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_52ac] ## Optional Lab: Gradient Descent Implementation for Logistic Regression
%[text] In this lab we will:
%[text] - Implement the gradient descent (GD) for logistic regression.
%[text] - Explore GD on a familiar dataset \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4d7e] ### Data set
%[text] Let's start with the same two feature data set used in the decision boundary lab.
X_train_2 = [0.5 1.5; 1 1; 1.5 0.5; 3 0.5; 2 2; 1 2.5];
y_train_2 = [0; 0; 0; 1; 1; 1];
%[text] Let's plot this data using the helper function `hBinaryScatter`,
f = figure; %[output:6e11bbcd]
hBinaryScatter(X_train_2, y_train_2, {'o', 'x'}, {'b', 'r'}, 8); %[output:6e11bbcd]
f.Position = [0 0 450 350]; %[output:6e11bbcd]
legend('y = 0','','','y = 1') %[output:6e11bbcd]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_723f] ### Logistic Gradient Descent
%[text] - $w\_j \n\n= w\_j - \\alpha\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}J(\\mathbf{w},b)\n\n= w\_j - \\alpha\\frac{1}{m}\\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\left( \\frac{1}{1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}} -  y^{(i)} \\right) \\cdot x\_j^{(i)} \n\n=  w\_j - \\alpha\\frac{1}{m}\\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( e^{(i)} \\cdot x\_j^{(i)} \\bigg)$
%[text] - $b \n\n= b - \\alpha\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b}J(\\mathbf{w},b)\n\n= b - \\alpha \\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( \\frac{1}{1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}} - y^{(i)} \\bigg) \n\n= b - \\alpha \\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} e^{(i)}$ \
%[text] for $i \\in \\{ 1,2,\\cdots,m\\}$ and $j \\in \\{ 1,2,\\cdots, n\\}$, where $m$ and $n$ are the number of samples and number of features, respectively. 
%[text] Similarly, the cost function is calculated as follows:
%[text] $J(\\mathbf{w},b) \n\n= \\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} L \\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} (\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}), y^{(i)}  \\right) \n\n= \\frac{1}{m} \\sum\_{i=1}^{m} \\bigg( - y^{(i)}\\log \\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) - (1-y^{(i)}) \\log \\left( 1 - f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}) \\right) \\bigg) \~,$
%[text] where $f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\frac{1}{1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}} \~ .$
w_init = [2; 3];
b_init = 1;
[J_vec, w, b] = hLogisticRegression(X_train_2, y_train_2, w_init, b_init, LearnRate=0.1, MaxIter=1); %[output:6fff7c2b]
%[text] As we made sure that the gradient descent (GD) with logistic regression works for a small scale example, we can do a full training and check the results as follows.
w_init = [0; 0]; % zero initialization
b_init = 0; % zero initialization
alpha = 0.1;
iters = 10000;
[J_vec, w, b] = hLogisticRegression(X_train_2, y_train_2, w_init, b_init, LearnRate=alpha, MaxIter=iters); %[output:97d754c7]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_77d8] ### **Let's now plot the results of the gradient descent**
%[text] In order to do the prediction with the parameters we've found, we need a threshold to make a decision such that:
%[text] $y = \n\\left\\{\n    \\begin{array}{lr}\n        1, & \\text{if } f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq \\text{Threshold}  \\\\\n        0, & \\text{if } f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}) \< \\text{Threshold}\n    \\end{array}$
%[text] where the threshold is chosen to be `0.5` since function of interest (the curve that we fit into our data) is "S-shaped" curve called **sigmoid function**. We can also express the above expression as follows:
%[text] $y = \n\\left\\{\n    \\begin{array}{lr}\n        1, & \\text{if } g(z) \\geq 0.5  \\\\\n        0, & \\text{if } g(z) \< 0.5\n    \\end{array}$    .
%[text] We can look at the sigmoid function plot and infer the following:
%[text] $y = \n\\left\\{\n    \\begin{array}{lr}\n        1, & \\text{if } z \\geq 0  \\\\\n        0, & \\text{if } z \< 0\n    \\end{array}$ 
%[text] where $z =  \\mathbf{x}\\mathbf{w} + b$,
%[text] $y = \n\\left\\{\n    \\begin{array}{lr}\n        1, & \\text{if } \\mathbf{x}\\mathbf{w} + b \\geq 0  \\\\\n        0, & \\text{if } \\mathbf{x}\\mathbf{w} + b \< 0\n    \\end{array}$ 
%[text] In order to visualize the above expression (decision boundary), we need to plot the line $\\mathbf{x}\\mathbf{w} + b = w\_1x\_1 + w\_2x\_2 +b = 0$.
f = figure; %[output:207f0e64]

% Plot the data
hBinaryScatter(X_train_2, y_train_2, {'o', 'x'}, {'b', 'r'}, 8); %[output:207f0e64]
% Plot the decision boundary
hold on %[output:207f0e64]
line([0 -b/w(1)],[-b/w(2) 0],'Color','green') %[output:207f0e64]
area([0 -b/w(1)],[-b/w(2) 0],'EdgeColor','green','FaceColor','blue','FaceAlpha',0.04) %[output:207f0e64]
hold off %[output:207f0e64]
f.Position = [0 0 450 350]; %[output:207f0e64]
legend('y = 0','','','y = 1','','','Decision boundary') %[output:207f0e64]
%[text] Note that in the above figure the decision boundary is the line at which the probability of sample being a member of class 1 is 0.5
%[text] - $f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(x) = P(y=1 \~|\~ \\mathbf{x};\\mathbf{w},b) = 0.5$ \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5b6e] ### Another Data Set
%[text] Let's now return to a one-dimensional data set. With just two parameters ($\\mathbf{w}$ and $b$), it is possible to plot the cost function using a contour plot get a better idea of what gradient descent does.
X_train_3 = [0 1 2 3 4 5].';
y_train_3 = [0 0 0 1 1 1].';
%[text] Let's plot the data points.
figure; %[output:4e71e18b]
hBinaryScatter(X_train_3, y_train_3, {'o','x'},{'b','r'}, 10) %[output:4e71e18b]
xlabel('Tumor size') %[output:4e71e18b]
ylabel('y') %[output:4e71e18b]
legend('Benign','','','Malignant','','') %[output:4e71e18b]
%[text] Let's visualize the gradient descent per each step, where we can track the linear regression, logistic regression, and cost function curves
iters = 8185; % number of training iterations %[control:slider:34af]{"position":[9,13]}

w_vec_range = -1:0.1:7;
b_range = -14:0.1:1;
alpha = 0.1;
w_init = 0;
b_init = 0;
[J_vec, w_vec, b_vec] = hInteractiveLogisticRegression(X_train_3, y_train_3, w_init, b_init, LearnRate=alpha, MaxIter=iters, Verbose=false); %[output:8c2c8957] %[output:66cfe044]
fprintf('Final values - Cost: %.4f | w: %.4f | b: %.4f\n ', J_vec(end), w_vec(end), b_vec(end)); %[output:09431daa]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_93c4] ## Appendix
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8d0d] ### 1 - Derivative of the sigmoid function with respect to the weights and a bias
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5dac] #### 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7412] #### Weights
%[text] As a direct consequence of the Appendix 2. We can infer the following:
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\right) \n\n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}\\left( \\frac{1}{1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}} \\right)\n\n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-1} \\\\\n\n= -\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-2} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)\n\n= -\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-2} \\cdot \\left( \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}\\big( -(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b) \\big) \\cdot e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right) \\\\\n\n= -\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-2} \\cdot e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\cdot \\bigg( \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}\\big( -\\left( x\_1^{(i)}w\_1+\\cdots+x\_j^{(i)}w\_j+\\cdots+x\_n^{(i)}w\_n + b \\right) \\big) \\bigg) \\\\\n\n=  -\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-2} \\cdot e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}  \\cdot \\left( -x\_j^{(i)} \\right) \\\\\n\n= \\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-2} \\cdot \\left( e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} +1-1\\right)  \\cdot \\left( x\_j^{(i)} \\right) \\\\\n\n=  x\_j^{(i)}\\frac{\\left( e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} +1-1\\right)}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{2}}\n\n=  x\_j^{(i)}\\frac{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}-1\\right)}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{2}}\n\n= x\_j^{(i)}\\frac{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{2}} - x\_j^{(i)}\\frac{1}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{2}} \\quad ,$
%[text] Therefore,
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\right)\n\n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_j}\\left( \\frac{1}{1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}} \\right) \\\\\n\n= \\frac{x\_j^{(i)}}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}\\right)} \\bigg( 1 - \\frac{1}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}\\right)} \\bigg)\n\n=   x\_j^{(i)} \\cdot f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\cdot \\big( 1- f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big) \\quad \\\#$
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_043e] #### 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7705] #### Bias
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b}\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\right)\n\n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b}\\left( \\frac{1}{1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}} \\right)\n\n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b}\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-1} \\\\\n\n= -\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-2} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)\n\n= -\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-2} \\cdot \\bigg( \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\big( -(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b) \\big) \\cdot e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\bigg) \\\\\n\n= -\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-2} \\cdot e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\cdot \\bigg( \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b} \\big( -\\left( x\_1^{(i)}w\_1 + x\_2^{(i)}w\_2 + \\cdots + x\_n^{(i)}w\_n + b \\right) \\big) \\bigg) \\\\\n\n=  -\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-2} \\cdot e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\cdot \\left( -1 \\right)\n\n= \\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^{-2} \\cdot e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\quad ,$
%[text] Therefore,
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b}\\left( f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\right)\n\n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial b}\\left( \\frac{1}{1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}} \\right)\n\n= \\frac{e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)^2} \\\\\n\n= \\frac{e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)} \\cdot  \\frac{1}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)} = \\frac{e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}+1-1}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)} \\\\\n\n= \\bigg( \\frac{1 + e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)}}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)} - \\frac{1}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)} \\bigg) \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)} \\\\\n\n= \\bigg( 1 -  \\frac{1}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)} \\bigg)  \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\left( 1+e^{-(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w}+b)} \\right)} \\\\\n\n= \\big( 1 - f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big) \\cdot  f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \n\n=  f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\cdot \\big( 1 - f\_{\\mathbf{w},b} \\left( \\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \\right) \\big) \\quad \\\#$
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3583] ### 2 - Derivative of the sigmoid function
%[text] In AI/ML area, the sigmoid function is defined as $\\sigma(x) = \\frac{1}{1+e^{-x}}$. Derivative of the sigmoid function is crucial for computing the updating the parameters in GD algorithm. Accordingly, in this section we will derive the derivative expression of the sigmoid function.
%[text] $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\sigma(x) =  \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x} \\frac{1}{\\left(1+e^{-x}\\right)} = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x} \\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right)^{-1} = -\\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right)^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x} \\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right) \\\\\n=  -\\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right)^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x} \\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right) = -\\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right)^{-2}\\left( -e^{-x} \\right) \\quad ,$
%[text] Therefore,
%[text] $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\sigma(x) = -\\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right)^{-2}\\left( -e^{-x} \\right) = \\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right)^{-2}\\left( e^{-x} +1-1 \\right) = \\frac{\\left(1+e^{-x}\\right)-1}{ \\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right)^{2} } \\\\\n= \\frac{1}{ \\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right)}  - \\frac{1}{\\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right)^{2}} = \\frac{1}{ \\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right)} \\bigg( 1-\\frac{1}{\\left( 1+e^{-x} \\right)} \\bigg) \\quad ,$
%[text] Finally,
%[text] $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\sigma(x) = \\sigma(x) \\big( 1-\\sigma(x) \\big) \\quad \\\#$
